course: dictionary
lesson: 5038
title: Injury: harm to the body
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An injury is harm done to a person's or animal's body, such as a cut, a bruise, a broken bone or a burn. Injure is the verb (to hurt someone), and injured is the adjective (hurt). Injury is a noun, so you can have an injury, several injuries, a serious injury or a minor injury.
explain: Look at the words around injury to find clues. «A&E, Accident and Emergency, is for serious injuries and illnesses.» Serious tells you an injury can be big and dangerous. «Urgent treatment centres deal with sprains, small cuts and minor burns» tells you that minor means small, so a minor injury is a small one that does not need the hospital's emergency room.
explain: The size of an injury is not always the same as how it looks. «A bump on the head swells because the scalp has many blood vessels that leak under the skin; the size of the bump does not show how serious the injury is.» A bump can look big and still be a small injury, or look small and be serious. That is why you should tell an adult about any injury.
fact: An injury is harm done to the body, such as a cut, a bruise, a broken bone or a burn.
fact: «Injury» is a noun, «injure» is the verb, and «injured» is the adjective.
fact: «Serious» injuries need urgent help; «minor» injuries are small, such as small cuts and minor burns.
fact: Many fish can regrow damaged fins, so an injury to a fin does not always last for ever.
fact: Hidden or played-down pain can mean an injury is found late, so always tell an adult when you are hurt.
exercise: «A&E, Accident and Emergency, is for serious injuries and illnesses.» What does serious tell you about an injury? | A tiny scratch that needs no help | A joke that makes people laugh | *A big harm that needs urgent help
exercise: «Urgent treatment centres deal with sprains, small cuts and minor burns.» Find and copy one word from the sentence that means small. | *minor | centres | burns | urgent
exercise: Sam says an injury is only a broken bone. What does an injury really mean? | A bruise is not an injury | *Harm done to the body, such as a cut, a bruise, a broken bone or a burn | Only harm to a person's feelings
test: «A bump on the head swells... the size of the bump does not show how serious the injury is.» What does this tell you? | A bump on the head is never an injury | A big bump is always a serious injury | *A small-looking bump can still be a serious injury
test: «Pain that is hidden or played down can mean an injury is found late and heals worse.» Why should you tell an adult about pain? | Because adults like to hear about pain | Because pain always means you must stop school | *So the injury can be found and treated early
exam: What helps prevent injuries in play and sport? | Running on wet, slippery floors without looking | Ignoring a cut because it looks small | *Wearing helmets and pads | Playing when you are hurt
